<interrogationes unitas="volumen">

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is a volumen and what is inside the file?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_creare volumen_acta_legere volumen_plagulas_enumerare" via="lib/volumen.worklog.md">One
      sqlite file that holds a whole document, so copying the file
      copies the document. Inside are three tables of its own (plus
      scrinium's _migrationes bookkeeping): acta, an
      append-only event log that is the truth; massae, content blobs
      keyed by SHA-256 hex (identical content is stored once); and
      plagulae, the manifest from path to sigillum, a projection that
      can be rebuilt from acta plus massae.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I store a file in a volumen and read it back?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_aperire_aut_creare volumen_plagulam_condere volumen_plagulam_promere volumen_claudere">Open
      with volumen_aperire_aut_creare(piscina, via), store with
      volumen_plagulam_condere(vol, via_relativa, contentum, origo)
      (one transaction: blob, plagula-condita event, manifest upsert),
      and read with volumen_plagulam_promere(vol, via_relativa,
      piscina, &amp;inventum). The result is a chorda in YOUR piscina,
      binary-safe and NOT NUL-terminated; *inventum separates an
      unknown path (FALSUM) from a file that is legitimately empty
      (VERUM, mensura 0). Finish with volumen_claudere.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass an empty origo string to volumen_plagulam_condere?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_plagulam_condere chorda_ex_literis">No,
      it returns FALSUM. The origo text is copied with
      chorda_ex_literis, which gives a NIHIL datum for "" (piscina
      refuses zero-byte allocations); that binds as SQL NULL and the
      origo column is NOT NULL. Pass a real label such as "genita" or
      "vendicata:lib/chorda.c".</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I get a reason when volumen_creare or volumen_aperire returns NIHIL?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_creare volumen_aperire volumen_error">Only
      on stderr. volumen_creare refuses a path that already exists and
      volumen_aperire one that does not, each printing one line and
      returning NIHIL; volumen_error(NIHIL) just says "volumen NIHIL".
      If volumen_creare fails after sqlite has made the file (the
      volumen-creatum event not written), the file stays on disk and
      every retry is refused as already existing. Once a Volumen is
      open, volumen_error gives the last sqlite
      message after a FALSUM or 0 return.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write several files atomically?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_transactionem_incipere volumen_transactionem_committere volumen_transactionem_revolvere volumen_plagulam_condere">Wrap
      the calls in volumen_transactionem_incipere and
      volumen_transactionem_committere (or _revolvere on failure).
      Inside them volumen_plagulam_condere and
      volumen_plagulam_removere do not open their own transaction and
      do not roll back on error: the caller owns it. Nesting is
      refused: a second incipere returns FALSUM, because sqlite
      cannot nest transactions.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I see what the document looked like at an earlier point?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_plicam_ad volumen_acta_legere volumen_massam_promere">volumen_plicam_ad(vol,
      seq, piscina) rebuilds the manifest by replaying the
      plagula-condita and plagula-remota events with seq &lt;= seq
      (seq &lt;= 0 means all, and then equals
      volumen_plagulas_enumerare, which the test pins); read the old content with
      volumen_massam_promere on each sigillum_hex.
      volumen_acta_legere(vol, post_seq, piscina) returns the events
      AFTER post_seq (exclusive; 0 = all), in seq order. Events whose
      JSON does not parse are skipped by the fold, not
      fatal.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does removing a file not free its content?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_plagulam_removere volumen_massam_condere volumen_summa_massarum">volumen_plagulam_removere
      appends a plagula-remota event and deletes the manifest row, but
      the blob stays in massae: the history still points at it, and
      volumen_plicam_ad at an earlier seq shows the file again. Removing
      a path that is not there still returns VERUM (idempotent) and
      still appends a plagula-remota event. There
      is no garbage collection of blobs.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I use a volumen in a test without touching the user's real files?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_temporarium volumen_claudere volumen_via">volumen_temporarium(piscina,
      praefixum) creates /tmp/praefixum-N.volumen with the first free N
      from 1 to C, and volumen_claudere DELETES that file. A test that
      forgets to close leaves its file behind, and after C leftovers
      volumen_temporarium returns NIHIL, so close every one.
      volumen_via tells you which file is really in use.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I record my own events in a volumen?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_actum_appendere volumen_acta_legere VolumenActum">volumen_actum_appendere(vol,
      genus, datum) appends to acta and returns the new seq (0 means
      failure). datum is meant to be JSON but is not checked; an empty
      genus, or an empty datum whose pointer is NIHIL (what
      chorda_ex_literis gives for ""), binds as SQL NULL and the NOT
      NULL columns refuse it; the
      timestamp (momentum, ISO UTC with milliseconds) is stamped by
      sqlite, not by C. Store image or other binary payloads with
      volumen_massam_condere and put the returned hex sigillum in the
      event's JSON: a blob is content, the event says how it is
      used.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep one Volumen open for the whole life of an application?
    <responsum symbolum="volumen_plagulam_condere volumen_actum_appendere volumen_aperire">Yes,
      but every write copies small strings (genus, sigillum hex, origo,
      the event JSON) into the piscina given at open, and an arena
      never frees them, so that piscina grows with each write. Read
      results go into the piscina you pass to each read call instead.
      The sqlite statements themselves are allocated by sqlite, not
      the piscina.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
